      "output": "**Water** is a simple chemical compound that is essential for life on Earth. Here’s a concise overview of what water is, how it’s structured, and why it matters:\n\n---\n\n## 1. Chemical Identity\n- **Formula:** **H₂O** – each molecule consists of two hydrogen atoms covalently bonded to one oxygen atom.\n- **Molar mass:** ≈ 18.015 g mol⁻¹.\n- **Bond angle:** ≈ 104.5°, giving the molecule a bent (V‑shaped) geometry.\n\n## 2. Physical States\n| State | Typical Conditions | Key Characteristics |\n|-------|--------------------|----------------------|\n| **Solid (Ice)** | ≤ 0 °C (32 °F) at 1 atm | Crystalline lattice, less dense than liquid water (hence ice floats). |\n| **Liquid** | 0 °C – 100 °C (32 °F – 212 °F) at 1 atm | Highly cohesive, excellent solvent, high surface tension, high specific heat capacity. |\n| **Gas (Steam/Vapor)** | ≥ 100 °C (212 °F) at 1 atm | Low density, expands to fill containers; can exist as invisible vapor or visible steam when condensed. |\n\n## 3. Unique Physical & Chemical Properties\n| Property | Why It’s Important |\n|----------|-------------------|\n| **Polarity** | The uneven charge distribution makes water a superb solvent for ionic and polar substances (e.g., salts, sugars, proteins). |\n| **Hydrogen bonding** | Weak bonds between water molecules give rise to high boiling point, high heat of vaporization, surface tension, and the anomalous density maximum at 4 °C. |\n| **High specific heat (4.18 J g⁻¹ °C⁻¹)** | Buffers temperature changes in oceans, lakes, and living organisms. |\n| **High heat of vaporization (≈ 2260 J g⁻¹)** | Drives cooling processes like sweating and transpiration. |\n| **Cohesion & adhesion** | Enables capillary action (water moving up plant xylem) and contributes to phenomena like meniscus formation. |\n| **Density anomaly** | Ice is ~9 % less dense than liquid water, allowing it to float and insulate aquatic life in winter. |\n\n## 4. Role in the Environment & Biology\n- **Hydrologic Cycle:** Evaporation → condensation → precipitation → runoff → infiltration → repeat. Water moves energy and matter around the planet.\n- **Solvent for Biochemistry:** Most metabolic reactions occur in aqueous solutions; water participates directly in hydrolysis, condensation, and redox reactions.\n- **Structural Component:** ~60 % of the human body (by weight) is water; it fills cells, transports nutrients, and regulates temperature.\n- **Ecosystem Support:** Aquatic habitats, soil moisture, and atmospheric humidity all depend on water’s presence and movement.\n\n## 5. Sources & Availability\n- **Freshwater:** Rivers, lakes, glaciers, groundwater (≈ 2.5 % of Earth’s water, but only ~0.3 % is readily accessible).\n- **Saltwater:** Oceans (≈ 97 % of total water) – not directly drinkable without desalination.\n- **Atmospheric water:** Water vapor and clouds, a small but dynamic reservoir.\n\n## 6. Everyday Forms & Uses\n- **Drinking water:** Essential for hydration and health.\n- **Industrial:** Coolants, solvents, steam for power generation.\n- **Agricultural:** Irrigation, livestock watering.\n- **Domestic:** Cooking, cleaning, bathing.\n\n---\n\n### Bottom Line\nWater is a tiny, polar molecule (H₂O) whose ability to form hydrogen bonds gives it a suite of extraordinary physical properties. These properties make water the universal solvent, a thermal regulator, and a key player in virtually every geological, chemical, and biological process on Earth. Its presence in liquid form is a defining factor for life as we know it."
